\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description \
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSCalendarDate is a public subclass of NSDate that creates concrete date objects for western calendars. These objects have time zones and calendar formats as attributes and are especially suited for representing and manipulating dates according to western calendrical systems. \
\fs14\
\fs28 An NSCalendarDate object stores a date as the number of seconds relative to the absolute reference date (the first instance of 1 January 2001, GMT). It uses an NSTimeZone object to adjust the visible representation of that date to reflect its associated time zone. The time zone object only affects the way the date is displayed; it does not affect the value stored. Because the value is stored independent of the time zone, you can accurately compare NSCalendarDates with any other NSDate objects or use them to create other NSDate objects. It also means that you can track an NSCalendarDate object across different time zones; that is, as you can change the NSTimeZone object to see how the particular date is represented in that time zone. \
\fs14\
\fs28 NSCalendarDate provides both class and instance methods for creating objects. Some of these methods allow you to initialize NSCalendarDate objects from strings while others create objects from sets of integers corresponding the standard time values (months, hours, seconds, and so on). \
\fs14\
\fs28 To retrieve conventional elements of an NSCalendarDate object, use the\b  ...Of...\b0  methods. For example, \b dayOfWeek\b0  returns a number that indicates the day of the week (0 is Sunday). The \b monthOfYear\b0  method returns a number between 1 and 12 that indicates the month.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSCalendarDate performs date computations based on western calendrical systems, primarily the Gregorian. (The algorithms are derived from public domain software described in \'aaCalendrical Calculations,\'ba a two-part series by Nachum Dershowitz and Edward M Reingold in \i Software \'d0 Practice and Experience\i0 ). \
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql The Calendar Format\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Each NSCalendarDate object has a calendar format associated with it. This format is a string that contains date-conversion specifiers that are very similar to those used in the standard C library function \b strftime()\b0 . NSCalendarDate interprets dates that are represented as strings conforming to this format. You can set the default format for an NSCalendarDate object at initialization time or using the \b setCalendarFormat:\b0  method. Several methods allow you to specify formats other than the one bound to the object.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The date conversion specifiers cover a range of date conventions:\
\fs14\
\pard \s60 \li6753 \fi-4838 \ri1007 \ql \tx6753 \tx9525 \fs28 %% \tab a '%' character\
\fs14\
\fs28 %a \tab abbreviated weekday name\
\fs14\
\fs28 %A \tab full weekday name\
\fs14\
\fs28 %b \tab abbreviated month name\
\fs14\
\fs28 %B \tab full month name\
\fs14\
\fs28 %c \tab shorthand for %X %x, the locale format for date and time\
\fs14\
\fs28 %d \tab day of the month as a decimal number (01-31) \
\fs14\
\fs28 %e\tab same as %d but does not print the leading 0 for days 1 through 9 \
\fs14\
\fs28 %F\tab milliseconds as a decimal number (000-999)\
\fs14\
\fs28 %H \tab hour based on a 24-hour clock as a decimal number (00-23) \
\fs14\
\fs28 %I \tab hour based on a 12-hour clock as a decimal number (01-12)\
\fs14\
\fs28 %j \tab day of the year as a decimal number (001-366) \
\fs14\
\fs28 %m \tab month as a decimal number (01-12) \
\fs14\
\fs28 %M \tab minute as a decimal number (00-59) \
\fs14\
\fs28 %p \tab AM/PM designation for the locale\
\fs14\
\fs28 %S \tab second as a decimal number (00-59) \
\fs14\
\fs28 %w \tab weekday as a decimal number (0-6), where Sunday is 0\
\fs14\
\fs28 %x \tab date using the date representation for the locale\
\fs14\
\fs28 %X \tab time using the time representation for the locale\
\fs14\
\fs28 %y \tab year without century (00-99) \
\fs14\
\fs28 %Y \tab year with century (such as 1990)\
\fs14\
\fs28 %Z \tab time zone abbreviation (such as PDT)\
\fs14\
\fs28 %z \tab time zone offset in hours and minutes from GMT (HHMM)\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s58 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql NSString Representations for NSCalendarDates\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSCalendarDate provides several \b description...\b0  methods for representing dates as strings. These methods\'d0\b description\b0 , \b descriptionWithLocale:\b0 , \b descriptionWithCalendarFormat:\b0 , and \b descriptionWithCalendarFormat:locale:\b0 \'d0take an implicit or explicit calendar format. The user\'27s locale information affects the returned string. NSCalendarDate accesses the locale information as an NSDictionary. If you use \b descriptionWithLocale:\b0  or \b descriptionWithCalendarFormat:locale:\b0 , you can specify a different locale dictionary. The following keys in the locale dictionary affect NSCalendarDates: \
\fs14\
\pard \s60 \li6753 \fi-4838 \ri1007 \ql \tx6753 \tx9525 \fs28 NSTimeDateFormatString\tab Specifies how dates with times are printed, affecting strings that use the format specifiers %c, %X, or %x. The default is to use abbreviated months and days with a 24 hour clock, as in \'aaSun Jan 01 23:00:00 +6 2001\'ba.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSAMPMDesignation\tab Specifies how the morning and afternoon designations are printed, affecting strings that use the %p format specifier. The default is AM and PM.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSMonthNameArray\tab Specifies the names for the months, affecting strings that use the %B format specifier.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSShortMonthNameArray\tab Specifies the abbreviations for the months, affecting strings that use the %b format specifier.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSWeekDayNameArray\tab Specifies the names for the days of the week, affecting strings that use the %A format specifier.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSShortWeekDayNameArray\tab Specifies the abbreviations for the days of the week, affecting strings that use the %a format specifier.\
\fs14\
\pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 \fs28 For more information on the locale dictionary, see the file \b Locales.rtf\b0  in the \i Foundation Framework Reference\i0 .\
\fs14\
