\fs14\
\fs28 For example, let\'27s say your company\'27s convention for dates on correspondence takes the form \'aaFriday, 1 July 1994, 11:45 AM.\'ba To get an NSCalendarDate object with a temporal value corresponding to this string, you would use this statement: \
\fs14\
\pard \s20 \li1915 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx2413 \tx2889 \tx3421 \tx3953 \tx4457 \tx4933 \tx7005 \tx7509 \tx8013 \tx8517 \tx9021 \tx9525 \tx10029 \tx10533 \tx11037 \tx11513 \f2 \fs24 NSCalendarDate *today = [NSCalendarDate \
\fi0 \tab dateWithString:@"Friday, 1 July 1994, 11:45 AM" \
\fi0 \tab calendarFormat:@"%A, %d %B %Y, %I:%M %p"];\
\fs14\
\f1 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 If you include a time zone in the \i description\i0  argument, this method verifies it and can substitute an alternative time zone. If the method does supply a new time zone, it applies the difference in offsets-from-GMT values between the substituted and the original time zone to the NSCalendarDate being created.\
